  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Mon May 11 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will slide from the Great Lakes region today \nto the central east coast Tuesday, providing dry conditions and\nlots of sunshine. Winds out of the north today will keep \ntemperatures seasonable in the middle to upper 70s. Winds will \nbecome southerly for Tuesday, and temperatures will be several \ndegrees warmer than today. \n\nModels bring a weak cold front through the PAH forecast area late \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning. Chances of showers\n(20-50%) and isolated thunderstorms can be expected along and \nahead of the front Tuesday evening and overnight, mainly \naffecting southern Illinois, southwest Indiana, and portions of \nsoutheast Missouri and west Kentucky north of a line from Cape \nGirardeau to Paducah to Hopkinsville. The highest chances will be\nacross southwest Indiana and portions of southern Illinois \nalong and north of Interstate 64. With limited moisture, \nrainfall amounts will generally top out at a few hundredths, \nwith many areas remaining dry or just seeing a few sprinkles. \n\nBehind the front on Wednesday, temperatures will remain near to a \nlittle above normal with cooler air lagging a little farther behind\nthe front. We will see breezy northwest winds through the \nafternoon with decreasing dew points, so it may at least feel a \nlittle cooler. It will be much cooler Wednesday night with \nlight to calm winds, clear skies and lows around 5 degrees \nbelow normal in the upper 40s to around 50 degrees. \n\nHigh pressure sinking south over the middle Mississippi and lower \nOhio valleys will keep conditions dry with lots of sunshine \nWednesday through Thursday.  The surface high will move to our east \nThursday night, and winds will shift to south, increasing on Friday. \nThese south winds will result in a warming trend Friday through the \nweekend and gradually increasing dew points. Highs will be in\nthe 80s Friday, then in the upper 80s over the weekend.\n\nAs for precipitation chances Friday through the weekend, timing\nof weak disturbances vary quite a bit between models. The model\nblend produces on and off 20-35% chances at best of showers and\nstorms, and pinpointing the best timing remains in question for\nnow.",
